SUMMARY:The Flying Ace w/live music and Q&A | Additional Films and Guests
DESCRIPTION:The Flying Ace is an aspirational "race film\," chronicling a
  Black WWI fighter pilot who\, after honorably serving\, returns to his c
 ivilian job as a railroad detective. He uses his military training and de
 tective skills to find and capture a gang of railroad thieves who stole $
 25\,000 from his company. Shot at a time when racism and segregation prev
 ented Black people from becoming pilots in the armed forces and when Holl
 ywood continually portrayed Black people as one-dimensional caricatures\,
  The Flying Ace empowers Black people and features multi-dimensional repr
 esentations of Black life\, culture\, and identities. The film was direct
 ed by Richard E. Norman and stars Laurence Criner\, Katherine Boyd\, and 
 Steve "Peg" Reynolds. The Flying Ace is the lone surviving film made at R
 ichard E. Norman's Jacksonville\, FL-based Norman Studios\, and this year
  marks the centennial of its release. Fully restored by the Library of Co
 ngress\, the film was selected for the National Film Registry in 2021. [6
 5 min\; mystery\, adventure\; silent with English title cards]
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><i>The Flying Ace</i> is an aspirational 
 "race film\," chronicling a Black WWI fighter pilot who\, after honorably
  serving\, returns to his civilian job as a railroad detective. He uses h
 is military training and detective skills to find and capture a gang of r
 ailroad thieves who stole $25\,000 from his company. Shot at a time when 
 racism and segregation prevented Black people from becoming pilots in the
  armed forces and when Hollywood continually portrayed Black people as on
 e-dimensional caricatures\, <i>The Flying Ace</i> empowers Black people a
 nd features multi-dimensional representations of Black life\, culture\, a
 nd identities. The film was directed by Richard E. Norman and stars Laure
 nce Criner\, Katherine Boyd\, and Steve "Peg" Reynolds. <i>The Flying Ace
 </i> is the lone surviving film made at Richard E. Norman's Jacksonville\
 , FL-based Norman Studios\, and this year marks the centennial of its rel
 ease. Fully restored by the Library of Congress\, the film was selected f
 or the National Film Registry in 2021. [65 min\; mystery\, adventure\; si
 lent with English title cards]</p>
