SUMMARY:The Battle of Algiers | The Long Revolution
DESCRIPTION:This visceral re-creation of a key year in the tumultuous Alg
 erian struggle for independence from their French colonizers in the 1950s
  remains a hallmark of revolutionary cinema. Recruited to join the Nation
 al Liberation Front\, Ali La Pointe quickly becomes involved in the revol
 ution\, where he and his compatriots battle the French state in the form 
 of Colonel Mathieu. This classic of Italian neorealist cinema is shot in 
 a documentary-inspired newsreel format with primarily non-professional ac
 tors who lived through the real events portrayed and soundtracked by a sc
 ore from Pontecorvo and Ennio Morricone. [121 min\; drama\, war\; Arabic 
 and French with English subtitles]
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<div class="collapsed individual-film__intro
  collapsed bg-none section" id="content"><div class="row"><div class="lay
 out"><div class="text"></div></div></div></div><div class="section bg-non
 e individual-film__intro"><div class="row"><div class="layout"><div class
 ="text"><p>          This visceral re-creation of a key year in the tumul
 tuous Algerian struggle for independence from their French colonizers in 
 the 1950s remains a hallmark of revolutionary cinema. Recruited to join t
 he National Liberation Front\, Ali La Pointe quickly becomes involved in 
 the revolution\, where he and his compatriots battle the French state in 
 the form of Colonel Mathieu. This classic of Italian neorealist cinema is
  shot in a documentary-inspired newsreel format with primarily non-profes
 sional actors who lived through the real events portrayed and soundtracke
 d by a score from Pontecorvo and Ennio Morricone. [121 min\; drama\, war\
 ; Arabic and French with English subtitles]        </p></div></div></div>
 </div>
